2. POINTER / NIL-MERGE SEMANTICS MATCH IsInternalTransfer ON BOTH PATHS,
   RESPONSE ECHO PRESENT — PASS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
Read from source (internal/services/mcpsvc/curate/upsert.go), not the manifest.

INPUT SHAPE (upsert.go:24) — byte-for-byte the IsInternalTransfer shape one
line above it, pointer type and omitempty included:
  IsInternalTransfer  *bool `json:"is_internal_transfer,omitempty" jsonschema:"treat matches as money moving between the user's own accounts, ..."`
  ExcludeFromPlanSync *bool `json:"exclude_from_plan_sync,omitempty" jsonschema:"leave unchanged if omitted; when true, the what-if dashboard sync excludes this expense's matched transactions from the synced living-expense figure because it is already modeled separately in the plan (e.g. a car loan as its own ExpenseSource)"`
The jsonschema description is present and states the nil-semantics explicitly
("leave unchanged if omitted"), as the criterion requires.

APPLIED ON BOTH CREATE AND EDIT — this is a structural property, and I
verified it structurally rather than trusting the claim. upsert.go:87-128:
  var (target models.MajorExpense; create = strings.TrimSpace(in.ID) == ""; found bool)
  if !create {                       // edit: target loaded from `existing`
      for _, e := range existing { if e.ID == in.ID { target, found = e, true; break } }
      ...
  }
  // ONE merge block, AFTER the create/edit branch, so it runs on both:
  if in.IsInternalTransfer != nil  { target.IsInternalTransfer  = *in.IsInternalTransfer  }
  if in.ExcludeFromPlanSync != nil { target.ExcludeFromPlanSync = *in.ExcludeFromPlanSync }
There is exactly ONE assignment to target.ExcludeFromPlanSync in the file, it
is nil-guarded, and it sits after the branch — so create (zero-value target)
and edit (loaded target) traverse the identical code. Immediately below,
upsert.go:150-157 dispatches to AddMajorExpense (create) or
UpdateMajorExpense (edit); SY1 already made UpdateMajorExpense copy this
field explicitly, so the edit path persists it.

RESPONSE ECHO (upsert.go:159-169):
  out = upsertOutput{ ID: target.ID, Created: create, Expense: majorExpenseRow{
        ... Notes: target.Notes, IsInternalTransfer: target.IsInternalTransfer,
        ExcludeFromPlanSync: target.ExcludeFromPlanSync, }, ... }
Echo present, sourced from the merged target.

NIL-vs-EXPLICIT-FALSE DISAMBIGUATION proven on actual JSON decoding, which is
the property the *bool exists for:
  $ go test -overlay=<probe> -count=1 -v -run TestProbeListRowSerializesFalse ./internal/services/mcpsvc/curate/
    PROBE-IN omitted -> nil pointer = true
    PROBE-IN explicit false -> non-nil pointer to false = true
Omitting the key yields nil (leave unchanged); sending `false` explicitly
yields a non-nil pointer to false (overwrite). The two are distinguishable,
which is exactly what a plain `bool` could not do.

TOOL DESCRIPTION: registerUpsert's prose gained one sentence for
exclude_from_plan_sync placed adjacent to the existing is_internal_transfer
sentence (upsert.go:64-70), so the model-facing description covers it too.

